02:30:09.940 --> 02:30:11.220
Curried function?

02:30:11.220 --> 02:30:12.120
Curried.

02:30:12.120 --> 02:30:15.900
Add five is, yes, that's curried because it's

02:30:15.900 --> 02:30:18.340
a single argument.

02:30:18.340 --> 02:30:21.720
So all of the ones on here that have single arguments

02:30:21.720 --> 02:30:22.140
are curried.

02:30:22.139 --> 02:30:34.859
So what this is pointing out though, if I have functions like this defined, like add1,

02:30:34.859 --> 02:30:38.920
see how there's different ways to do it, you can actually get partial application by doing

02:30:38.920 --> 02:30:45.139
this underscore here. So syntactically, that will get you partial application. You can do that here

02:30:45.139 --> 02:30:50.719
as well. This is with the multiple parameter lists, the current version. Add3P, we're back

02:30:50.719 --> 02:30:59.279
to the case where we need to put the underscore here. Add 4p. In this case, because this thing

02:30:59.279 --> 02:31:04.659
returns a function, you can take this function back and you don't have to do the underscore

02:31:04.659 --> 02:31:10.079
thing. That's what's going on there. So if I have this code here, so actually this will

02:31:10.079 --> 02:31:16.319
like... Oh, it doesn't show you everything. Okay. So if I do this... Oh yeah, it's all

02:31:16.319 --> 02:31:20.539
over there. Okay, so if I have my list of... Here's my... This is actually a list of functions,

02:31:20.540 --> 02:31:25.980
Right. So I have a list of add 1 P. Add 2 P. Add 3 P. Add 4 P. Add 5 P.

02:31:26.520 --> 02:31:28.900
Each of these is going to be a function. Right.

02:31:28.980 --> 02:31:31.980
So this will actually call apply on that function.

02:31:32.740 --> 02:31:34.900
It will apply the function. That's why it's called apply.

02:31:34.980 --> 02:31:38.180
Because we're applying a function. That's what we, that's how we say it in math.

02:31:38.700 --> 02:31:42.060
So we're going to say, all right, I'm going to call f of 21.

02:31:42.360 --> 02:31:44.860
So in each case of, you know, you can see it's pretty trivial.

02:31:44.860 --> 02:31:48.260
It's always going to take whatever argument and add to 11. Right.

02:31:48.260 --> 02:31:53.960
So that's why if we take this list of functions and then we map, because that's what this for yield is doing.

02:31:54.200 --> 02:31:58.020
We map to calling f, we're going to get 32 for each one.

02:31:59.639 --> 02:32:02.819
So that's kind of a summary of partial application.

02:32:06.300 --> 02:32:07.479
That's the .

