02:32:52.739 --> 02:32:54.500
But first let's talk about folds.

02:32:54.500 --> 02:32:55.340
What are they?

02:32:59.579 --> 02:33:01.899
How do we combine collection elements

02:33:01.899 --> 02:33:03.119
into an aggregate result?

02:33:03.119 --> 02:33:03.959
So that's what we've been doing right?

02:33:03.959 --> 02:33:05.520
With our maps, right?

02:33:07.000 --> 02:33:08.039
Our flat maps.

02:33:08.040 --> 02:33:12.660
like, now we're going to start expressing result aggregation

02:33:12.660 --> 02:33:15.640
using this new thing called a fold.

02:33:15.640 --> 02:33:17.840
And we'll talk about the difference between fold left

02:33:17.840 --> 02:33:20.800
and fold right.

02:33:20.800 --> 02:33:26.620
So these are common concepts in functional programming.

02:33:26.620 --> 02:33:30.980
All right, so here's a little exercise.

02:33:30.980 --> 02:33:37.120
So we'll express this by iterating through the list.

02:33:37.120 --> 02:33:40.520
In Java, we would do that something like this.

02:33:40.520 --> 02:33:41.640
We have some mutable memory.

02:33:41.640 --> 02:33:45.440
So again, our computation is stored here in this result

02:33:45.440 --> 02:33:45.940
variable.

02:33:45.940 --> 02:33:47.920
And then we iterate through the elements,

02:33:47.920 --> 02:33:51.680
and we add each element to the result.

02:33:51.680 --> 02:33:55.060
So that's the classic way we do sum.

02:33:55.060 --> 02:33:59.560
In Scala, this is the Scala version of that.

02:33:59.560 --> 02:34:04.380
Notice it adds this little ensuring contract here.

02:34:04.379 --> 02:34:07.659
So we don't have this filled in.

02:34:07.659 --> 02:34:10.059
So these three question marks if you haven't figured out yet

02:34:10.059 --> 02:34:12.779
through not implemented error, I think I said that before.

02:34:12.779 --> 02:34:13.920
But if you get your homework,

02:34:13.920 --> 02:34:17.639
it all has these little bombs waiting for you to like,

02:34:17.639 --> 02:34:19.420
they're not literal bombs, but they're like,

02:34:19.420 --> 02:34:22.000
you know, it throws an all pointer exception,

02:34:22.000 --> 02:34:24.719
so they're waiting for you to clean those up.

02:34:24.719 --> 02:34:28.939
So, yeah, so here it's the same idea.

02:34:28.939 --> 02:34:31.399
You'll have this mutable storage result,

02:34:31.399 --> 02:34:33.959
and then you're always adding each X

02:34:33.959 --> 02:34:45.599
to that result. This is a for do, so that's going to do a for each.

02:34:45.599 --> 02:34:59.019
We want a concise function to aggregate all of the elements of xs into a single result.

02:34:59.019 --> 02:35:03.399
We can express this with a recursive function. This is what your homework was last week,

02:35:03.399 --> 02:35:07.899
So, if I'm calling sum, and I get to the end of the list, well,

02:35:07.899 --> 02:35:11.559
the sum of that is 0, because that's the additive identity.

02:35:11.559 --> 02:35:16.699
And otherwise, I have some head value and some rest of the list.

02:35:16.699 --> 02:35:19.440
So, I take that head value, and

02:35:19.440 --> 02:35:22.539
I add it to the sum of the rest of the list, right?

02:35:22.539 --> 02:35:30.940
So, I just recurse on down, and if I do this, I end up with 63, I think.

02:35:30.940 --> 02:35:40.700
I think. So this is how the computation proceeds. I call sum with the list. I pulled that 11 off and

02:35:40.700 --> 02:35:48.620
I do 11 plus. I've seen this before. And then next time through, I pull the 21 off and I do this sum

02:35:49.180 --> 02:35:54.460
and then I pulled 31 off and I do this sum. And I get to the end, I get sum of nil. That's going

02:35:54.459 --> 02:36:00.399
going to be 0, so then I evaluate as the call stack exits and each function exits.

02:36:01.279 --> 02:36:02.739
I end up with 63.

02:36:02.939 --> 02:36:03.599
Oh, I can do math.

02:36:07.959 --> 02:36:11.699
What about if we want to use a different 0 element?

02:36:14.000 --> 02:36:18.479
So maybe, so this, I told you this last week, I think, or another week, that this kind of

02:36:18.479 --> 02:36:25.219
syntax here means if I call the sum function without passing the second argument, it will

02:36:25.219 --> 02:36:31.180
default the value to whatever I give it. So here when I call sum with excess, the second

02:36:31.180 --> 02:36:40.520
argument is zero because that's the default value. So here I have my excess. When I get

02:36:40.520 --> 02:36:47.520
to the empty list, I just take z, whatever z is. So if I'd called it with a thousand,

02:36:47.520 --> 02:36:53.020
then I would have sum the list but added 1,000 instead of 0.

02:36:53.020 --> 02:36:57.840
So that would be...it would be weird to do that because you wouldn't really maybe want that.

02:36:57.840 --> 02:37:01.100
You wouldn't want to call that sum, might want to call it special sum.

02:37:01.100 --> 02:37:02.640
I don't know what you want to call that.

02:37:02.640 --> 02:37:05.739
But so it's a little interesting.

02:37:05.739 --> 02:37:09.340
But again, this is somewhat contrived.

02:37:09.340 --> 02:37:14.420
But that's...you can do that with a 0 element.

02:37:14.420 --> 02:37:23.479
So if I call some 11 21 31 and get an empty list, then here I start with, I'm sorry, I

02:37:23.479 --> 02:37:24.879
end with an empty list.

02:37:24.879 --> 02:37:28.819
I don't get an empty list, I end with an empty list.

02:37:28.819 --> 02:37:35.120
So I kind of pull this 11 off and then I call recursively, pull the 21 off, call recursively,

02:37:35.120 --> 02:37:37.920
pull the 31 off, call recursively.

02:37:37.920 --> 02:37:43.600
Now I get down to here.

02:37:43.600 --> 02:37:46.940
So again, I'm going to get 63.

02:37:46.940 --> 02:37:51.940
This is the same as before, but the only difference is I added a different 0 element, so before

02:37:51.940 --> 02:37:57.059
I had hardcoded 0 on line 2.

02:37:57.059 --> 02:38:05.479
So now I'm going to take that idea because that's kind of useless in and of itself but

02:38:05.479 --> 02:38:09.180
I'm going to actually use that to my advantage.

02:38:09.180 --> 02:38:15.319
This is touching on Raven's question from earlier.

02:38:15.319 --> 02:38:18.760
This is tail recursive because there's no work to do here.

02:38:18.760 --> 02:38:22.880
We're actually pushing the state down here into the parameters.

02:38:24.260 --> 02:38:29.940
So in this case, when I have empty list, I just return whatever my z is.

02:38:29.940 --> 02:38:34.319
Here what I'm doing is I'm actually doing the addition here before I call recursively.

02:38:34.319 --> 02:38:39.559
So I'm gonna end up with, so this actually makes the z somewhat more compelling,

02:38:39.559 --> 02:38:40.079
the accumulator idea.

02:38:44.219 --> 02:38:50.319
So if I call this, notice now what's different,

02:38:50.319 --> 02:38:54.059
before we had the 11 out here with a plus, right?

02:38:54.059 --> 02:38:56.479
Now we have the 11 here.

02:38:56.479 --> 02:39:00.500
So this is why there's no more computation to do over here.

02:39:04.319 --> 02:39:08.399
So then now I've added 21 to 11 and passed it on down, right?

02:39:08.440 --> 02:39:09.739
So now I'm at 32.

02:39:10.860 --> 02:39:14.959
And now I'm adding 31 to 32 and passing it on down to 63.

02:39:15.340 --> 02:39:20.199
Now I match on the nil case, so then the 0 element is 63.

02:39:21.479 --> 02:39:23.539
And so it has these extra lines here.

02:39:24.319 --> 02:39:28.639
That's just because the other one, it had extra spaces, right?

02:39:28.639 --> 02:39:30.199
So it's just to keep it lined up.

02:39:30.299 --> 02:39:30.760
I don't know why.

02:39:31.719 --> 02:39:33.000
As soon as I saw that, I knew why.

02:39:33.079 --> 02:39:33.940
But I was like, that's weird.

02:39:33.940 --> 02:39:34.680
I didn't say this.

02:39:35.220 --> 02:39:37.659
You can just imagine those three lines out there.

02:39:38.600 --> 02:39:40.500
So that's like where it gets to the nil case

02:39:40.500 --> 02:39:41.640
and immediately returns 60.

02:39:43.860 --> 02:39:47.360
So this is, you know, the computer scientist will call this,

02:39:48.300 --> 02:39:50.720
will say that you're summing the elements in a list

02:39:50.720 --> 02:39:52.280
by computing forward.

02:39:52.680 --> 02:39:56.040
You're computing forward because you're doing this computation

02:39:56.040 --> 02:39:57.360
and then moving forward.

02:39:59.239 --> 02:40:01.300
I cover this a little bit in Data Structures.

02:40:01.500 --> 02:40:03.899
Although I never learned that in school.

02:40:03.940 --> 02:40:06.720
So like this computing forward and computing backwards.

02:40:06.720 --> 02:40:08.800
Although this is what they use in the literature

02:40:08.800 --> 02:40:11.540
when they talk about programming languages

02:40:11.540 --> 02:40:15.579
in the academic papers and stuff.

02:40:15.579 --> 02:40:19.680
So now we're going to generalize the plus operation.

02:40:19.680 --> 02:40:23.440
So we have this sum here.

02:40:23.440 --> 02:40:25.360
This is pretty much what we've been doing.

02:40:25.360 --> 02:40:26.319
We have our xs.

02:40:26.319 --> 02:40:27.180
We match it.

02:40:27.180 --> 02:40:27.780
We get nil.

02:40:27.780 --> 02:40:32.420
We return the z. Otherwise, we take x, add it to the z,

02:40:32.420 --> 02:40:36.620
and pass it on down as our new z to our recursive call.

02:40:39.379 --> 02:40:41.780
We're going to generalize the plus operation.

02:40:43.079 --> 02:40:47.600
And now instead, so see what we did here is we still have our xs list of int.

02:40:47.719 --> 02:40:49.979
We still have our z, which is an int.

02:40:50.059 --> 02:40:51.460
I'm going back and forth, by the way.

02:40:51.819 --> 02:40:54.920
The function name is changing from sum to fold left.

02:40:54.920 --> 02:40:56.219
Just look at the top line.

02:40:57.079 --> 02:40:59.340
The word sum is changing to fold left.

02:40:59.479 --> 02:41:00.700
And then we're adding a new parameter.

02:41:01.200 --> 02:41:05.220
The new parameter we're adding is whatever the operation is.

02:41:05.220 --> 02:41:11.620
So in this case, we have two ints that we combine somehow to produce a new int.

02:41:11.880 --> 02:41:16.200
So we're generalizing the addition operation by passing that in.

02:41:16.260 --> 02:41:18.880
We're abstracting out what the actual operation is.

02:41:19.300 --> 02:41:25.640
So instead of sum, which implies that we're actually hard-coding the sum,

02:41:26.260 --> 02:41:29.140
now what we're doing is we're saying,

02:41:29.139 --> 02:41:35.519
I'm going to abstract that out. I'm going to take that sum and I'm going to make that something that the caller passes in.

02:41:36.379 --> 02:41:41.979
So now this is why it's no longer called sum, it's called fold left.

02:41:42.299 --> 02:41:47.699
Why is it called fold left? Well, think about what happens.

02:41:48.260 --> 02:41:52.799
So if we get to the empty list, obviously we return whatever the Z is.

02:41:53.439 --> 02:41:59.779
Otherwise, we combine the first element, the head,

02:42:00.599 --> 02:42:03.179
with whatever the z we started with was.

02:42:03.500 --> 02:42:09.079
And then that new result is passed down to the next recursive call.

02:42:09.619 --> 02:42:11.979
And of course, we always have to pass the function down

02:42:11.979 --> 02:42:16.079
because as we call recursively, we have to have access to that function.

02:42:16.239 --> 02:42:17.859
So that has to be on the parameter list.

02:42:18.659 --> 02:42:21.379
But now we're going to process the rest of the list.

02:42:21.379 --> 02:42:32.379
So, notice what we are doing is, we are taking the elements from the left and doing the combination with the Z before recursing.

02:42:32.379 --> 02:42:37.379
So that's why it's called fold left. We're folding from the left.

02:42:37.379 --> 02:42:46.379
So if you memorize one thing, it's fold left means fold from the left and fold right means fold from the right. We'll talk about that later.

02:42:46.379 --> 02:42:53.319
So, this will give us the same result with sum of 63.

02:42:53.319 --> 02:42:59.479
So, what we've just done is we've implemented sum using bold left.

02:42:59.479 --> 02:43:09.279
Now, what I did here was I changed the elements and aggregate type.

02:43:09.279 --> 02:43:12.479
So, here, I was always dealing with ints.

02:43:12.479 --> 02:43:13.579
I had a list of ints.

02:43:13.579 --> 02:43:15.379
I had a z that was an int.

02:43:15.379 --> 02:43:18.459
I had a function that took two ints and returned an int.

02:43:19.159 --> 02:43:24.319
If I want my final result, which is over here, to be a string, for example,

02:43:24.939 --> 02:43:26.379
then the types need to change.

02:43:26.899 --> 02:43:27.699
So let's do that.

02:43:27.739 --> 02:43:29.420
We just change this to a string.

02:43:29.459 --> 02:43:33.199
But that means that our z, we have to start off with a string

02:43:33.199 --> 02:43:37.379
because we have to combine whatever that string was

02:43:37.379 --> 02:43:40.719
with whatever the next element is.

02:43:41.259 --> 02:43:43.779
So we have to take the string and the int and produce a string.

02:43:45.379 --> 02:43:47.959
So all I've done here, if you just look at the top line,

02:43:48.059 --> 02:43:51.420
is I've changed all of these places that have string now.

02:43:51.659 --> 02:43:52.539
They used to have int.

02:43:54.000 --> 02:43:54.279
Okay?

02:43:55.420 --> 02:43:57.179
But the structure is the same, right?

02:43:57.239 --> 02:43:59.159
So I do xs match.

02:43:59.479 --> 02:44:01.139
If I get nil, I get the z.

02:44:01.859 --> 02:44:07.679
If I have xs rest, then I'm going to do fold left on the rest of the list,

02:44:07.679 --> 02:44:13.379
but before I call that, I'm going to actually combine my new element x with my z

02:44:13.379 --> 02:44:14.879
to get a new string.

02:44:14.879 --> 02:44:17.619
that would be passed here for the new Z.

02:44:18.359 --> 02:44:20.679
And of course, I have to keep passing the function on down.

02:44:21.859 --> 02:44:24.599
So here, this illustrates nicely

02:44:24.599 --> 02:44:30.839
that if we make the Z element the exclamation point,

02:44:31.259 --> 02:44:34.920
that's what this call is, so we have our list,

02:44:35.379 --> 02:44:36.819
we have the exclamation point,

02:44:37.119 --> 02:44:38.879
and then we do a string concatenation

02:44:38.879 --> 02:44:43.139
where we take the string, and then a space,

02:44:43.139 --> 02:44:46.000
and then we add the int over here.

02:44:46.340 --> 02:44:48.019
This is actually going to end up being int.

02:44:48.659 --> 02:44:50.519
It'll call to string for you.

02:44:54.959 --> 02:44:56.920
Because we're folding from the left,

02:44:57.180 --> 02:44:59.799
we end up with the exclamation point on the left.

02:45:03.699 --> 02:45:06.559
So let's now abstract out the types.

02:45:06.659 --> 02:45:07.659
So what do we do here?

02:45:07.659 --> 02:45:11.059
Well, instead of ins and strings,

02:45:11.059 --> 02:45:18.459
we had z's and X's, so here we're fixing those types to int and string. Here we're

02:45:18.459 --> 02:45:24.379
saying okay caller you can use whatever type you want. So we say the zero

02:45:24.379 --> 02:45:28.439
element is the result, whatever the type of the zero element is going to be the

02:45:28.439 --> 02:45:32.840
type of the result of the call and then X is going to define the type of the

02:45:32.840 --> 02:45:40.379
input elements in the list. So what we've done now is we've abstracted out

02:45:40.379 --> 02:45:43.159
out the list is now a list of x's.

02:45:43.159 --> 02:45:46.979
And our z is now whatever type we define.

02:45:46.979 --> 02:45:49.459
But this has to conform.

02:45:49.459 --> 02:45:52.399
So this z type has to be here.

02:45:52.399 --> 02:45:53.659
And this x type has to be here.

02:45:53.659 --> 02:45:55.920
And when the combined function executes,

02:45:55.920 --> 02:45:59.739
it has to give us a new z.

02:45:59.739 --> 02:46:03.359
So if we deconstruct this, so if we have an empty list,

02:46:03.359 --> 02:46:05.799
we just return whatever our z was.

02:46:05.799 --> 02:46:07.399
That's why this is z.

02:46:07.399 --> 02:46:09.799
And then if we have anything else,

02:46:09.799 --> 02:46:14.100
we call fold left recursively on the rest of the list.

02:46:14.100 --> 02:46:17.959
But we do that only after we've combined our x with the z

02:46:17.959 --> 02:46:22.399
to produce a new type z, capital z.

02:46:22.399 --> 02:46:24.319
And of course passing the function.

02:46:24.319 --> 02:46:25.879
So here is what it looks like.

02:46:25.879 --> 02:46:29.760
If I do fold left like this, I get the same result

02:46:29.760 --> 02:46:30.840
as I had before.

02:46:30.840 --> 02:46:33.059
All I've done is I've changed the types here.

02:46:33.059 --> 02:46:36.719
I made the types abstract in the function,

02:46:36.719 --> 02:46:38.399
in the function definition.

02:46:42.239 --> 02:46:46.719
